1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 { We are at the starting position of the Rio de Janeiro Variation of the Berlin Defense. The origin of the name is mysterious, since in databases you will find only one game in this line played in the Brazilian city more than 100 years after the variation's birth (Tal-Panno, 1987). In fact, it is one of the oldest variations known in chess and was upheld by such household names as Steinitz, Anderssen, Lasker, Tarrasch, Pilsburry, Reti, Smyslov, Spassky and others. However, in the 21th century, following the trend set by Kasparov-Kramnik 2000 World Championship match it has given way to l'Hermit Variation: 5...Nd6, leading to the famous Berlin Endgame (also called 'Berlin Wall') Thanks to an overwhelming popularity of 5....Nd6 nowadays, it is very probable that your fifth move: 5... Be7 will already come as a surprise to a majority of White players. If you are already a mainstream Berlin player, it can serve as a great alternative line. However, this line is not only an excellent surprise weapon. After analysing it thoroughly, I am confident that it is solid enough to be used as a main weapon even against high-level opposition. } 6. Qe2 { An absolutely main move and the only really challenging one. -KEY- } Nd6 7. Bxc6 bxc6 8. dxe5 ( { Let me explain why the quirky } 8. dxe5 Nb7 { is better than the natural-looking } ) 
Nf5 $6 { The reason is very concrete. White has a powerful move at his disposal: } 9. Qe4 $1 { In this way, White forces a fatal weakening of the dark squares by } g6 { which renders the whole Black's position unattractive. After reinforcing his central control with } 10. c4 O-O 11. Nc3 { White is on a clear path to an advantage. } 
